WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
For a movie about dolls made out of plastic,&nbsp;
"Barbie" shows an unexpected heart and humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.740
If you were thrown by the big themes in this&nbsp;
story about toys, don't worry. We've got you.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.960
Warning: Spoilers for "Barbie" ahead!

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.880
﻿"Barbie" begins with Margot Robbie's&nbsp;
Barbie in Barbieland, the fantastical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.820
pastel-colored feminist utopia and home to every&nbsp;
Barbie who has ever lived. There's also Issa Rae's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.800
President Barbie, Hari Nef's Doctor Barbie,&nbsp;
Alexandra Shipp's Author Barbie, and so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.080
others. There are Kens, too. They're all named&nbsp;
Ken, including Ryan Gosling's Ken. Every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.120
Barbie has the best day of her life, while&nbsp;
Ken just hopes Barbie will look his way. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.140
Barbieland, Barbies are just being Barbie,&nbsp;
building one another up with praise and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.820
support and love, while Kens pine on the&nbsp;
outskirts, trying to outshine one another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.780
in the hopes of getting some attention&nbsp;
or respect. It is a never-ending cycle.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:57.120
But one day, Robbie Barbie's idyllic little&nbsp;
routine begins to crumble with strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.000
unfamiliar symptoms. Barbie discovers the issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.360
she's facing, including cellulite&nbsp;
and pervasive thoughts of death…

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.160
"Do you guys ever think about dying?"

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.200
…are the result of the tether between&nbsp;
Barbieland and the real world. Basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.040
the Barbies in Barbieland are tied to the&nbsp;
humans who play with them in the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.720
Whatever child is playing with this particular&nbsp;
Barbie is struggling with more complex issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.620
and they're poisoning her otherwise&nbsp;
perfect existence. The only way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.640
her to restore herself to factory settings&nbsp;
is to physically go into the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.540
a place that the residents of Barbieland&nbsp;
have some major misconceptions about.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.060
Once she traverses the theoretical wormhole&nbsp;
between the two realms with her stowaway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.980
Ken in tow, Barbie is immediately struck by the&nbsp;
power structure in the real world. Simply put,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:46.860
men rule the world here, and women do not, a&nbsp;
paradigm shift that perturbs Barbie but intrigues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.660
Ken. If that culture shock wasn't enough, when&nbsp;
she finally finds Sasha, the young girl Barbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.400
saw in her visions as the disaffected child&nbsp;
causing her issues, she is stunned to discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.460
that she and her Gen-Z friends hate Barbie. She's&nbsp;
shaken by the passionate, damning indictment they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.780
inflict upon her, noting Barbie's toxic&nbsp;
cultural footprint, crass consumerism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.060
and noxious effect on beauty standards, and by&nbsp;
extension women's mental wellness, over half a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.460
century. At her lowest point, Barbie is kidnapped&nbsp;
by Mattel agents at the order of their CEO.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.900
Everyone at Mattel just wants Barbie to&nbsp;
get back in a box and teleport back home.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.780
"Ken isn't something we're worried about."

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.460
But soon her and Ken's presence has the&nbsp;
same effect on the real world Sasha's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.800
cynicism has had on Barbie. Barbie flees and&nbsp;
is saved by Sasha's mother, Gloria, a Mattel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.300
employee who is revealed to be the real cause of&nbsp;
Barbie's plight. She had been playing with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.440
and her daughter's old dolls in an attempt to&nbsp;
reconnect with her grouchy, disaffected child.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.180
Barbie convinces them to join her back in&nbsp;
Barbieland, hoping the paisley majesty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.320
her home will inspire Sasha to be less&nbsp;
jaded and to stop hating Barbie as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.480
cultural icon. Perhaps it'll help repair Sasha&nbsp;
and Gloria's relationship in the process and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.440
at least on a smaller scale, fulfill some&nbsp;
of the destiny Barbie had thought already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.060
was cemented before her unfortunate sojourn&nbsp;
to the real world. When they actually make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.320
back to Barbieland, they discover it is&nbsp;
not what it once was. It is now…Kendom.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.960
Robbie's Barbie has no real allies among&nbsp;
the dolls, and doesn't feel special enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:21.240
to enact change with President Barbie and the&nbsp;
others having given into being housewives. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.920
our Barbie lays down and gives up. To escape&nbsp;
the macho madness that Barbieland has become,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.720
Gloria, Sasha, and Barbie hide at Weird&nbsp;
Barbie's compound. Gloria gives Barbie a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.400
dramatic and heartfelt speech about the inherent&nbsp;
difficulty of being a woman and it snaps Barbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.800
out of her doldrums. The gang deprograms Author&nbsp;
Barbie and puts together an elaborate plan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.880
do the same to the other Barbies, one by&nbsp;
one. The liberated Barbies will distract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.500
the Kens while Gloria gives the other Barbies&nbsp;
the talk that brings them back to their senses.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.840
Step two of this plan is to systematically deflate&nbsp;
the Kens' egos and set them against one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.880
continuing to hide in plain sight under the guise&nbsp;
of obedient housewives until the right time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.080
strike. Gosling's Ken is hurt by the sight&nbsp;
of his Barbie flirting with his arch-rival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.400
Simu Liu's Ken, leading the Kens to&nbsp;
go to literal war with each other.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.220
The initial violence and brutality don't last for&nbsp;
very long, though. Ken's solo song "I'm Ken" seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.600
to strike a chord within all the other Kens,&nbsp;
as their dance-fighting becomes something more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.400
collaborative. In spite of themselves, they've&nbsp;
stumbled upon the solidarity and brotherly love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.260
their short-sighted existences had been missing&nbsp;
out on this entire time. The war now over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.340
Barbieland reverts to normal — with&nbsp;
some added concessions for the Kens.﻿﻿

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.300
﻿
When Barbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.980
first gets to the real world, she's horrified&nbsp;
to discover its patriarchal structure. Ken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.360
however, isn't scared of it. He's elated. After&nbsp;
getting a bunch of library books about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:47.220
patriarchy from the library, he fails to get an&nbsp;
important job for the simple reason he is a man.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.240
"But I'm a man."

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:48.900
"But not a doctor."

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:49.920
"Can I talk to a doctor?"

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.120
"You are talking to a doctor."

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.780
Ken thus decides that the real&nbsp;
world is doing patriarchy wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.380
and he needs to take those ideas&nbsp;
somewhere new to fully realize them.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.480
So he functionally takes over Barbieland, teaching&nbsp;
all the other Kens to subjugate their respective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.280
Barbies. They turn the dream houses into man&nbsp;
caves. The choreographed disco parties become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:12.960
acoustic guitar covers of Matchbox Twenty's&nbsp;
"Push." The Kens convince every Barbie to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.240
being a girlboss and just pass her boy a brewski.&nbsp;
The Kens throw out the constitution and corrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.340
everything Barbieland once was, much to Barbie's&nbsp;
chagrin when she returns with Gloria and Sasha.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:27.120
Once the Barbies distract the Kens to&nbsp;
reinstate their constitution and return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.040
Barbieland to its former splendor, Ken&nbsp;
still doesn't know who he is or where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:36.540
belongs. He realizes he lost his enthusiasm&nbsp;
for patriarchy when he discovered it didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:41.460
involve horses to the degree he once imagined.&nbsp;
Barbie apologizes for the way she treated Ken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.480
but has to caution him that this isn't&nbsp;
a happy romantic ending for the two of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.800
them. She says he needs to spend time and&nbsp;
energy defining his own place in this world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:54.780
outside of trying to court her. President Barbie&nbsp;
agrees to give the Kens more representation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.560
their government. Meanwhile, the Mattel&nbsp;
execs who crossed the beach portal are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.520
satisfied, agreeing to start producing&nbsp;
Gloria's idea for an Everyday Barbie.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.920
Everyone gets some kind of conclusion they&nbsp;
find satisfying — that is, except for Robbie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.480
Barbie. She wonders aloud if there's anything&nbsp;
left for her to do, still feeling confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.740
and bothered by all she's experienced.&nbsp;
She still sees herself as too ordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:21.120
and unexceptional to deserve the kind of happy&nbsp;
ending a movie like this is supposed to have.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.220
﻿﻿But that's when Ruth Handler, the creator of&nbsp;
Barbie, played by RheaPearlman, appears. She's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:31.740
essentially God in Barbieland, and she offers&nbsp;
Barbie some holy counsel. Handler talks to Barbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:36.420
about the true nature of life and death, along&nbsp;
with what it means to be a human. Moved by all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:41.700
this, Barbie asks if she can be a human too. But&nbsp;
after all she's endured on this quirky adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.620
she finds she already is.
﻿&nbsp;

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.060
In the film's final moments, Barbie goes with&nbsp;
Gloria and her family to some important event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:53.700
with the tenor of the scene suggesting this will&nbsp;
be her first day at her first human job, perhaps a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:58.860
gig working at Mattel as a creative. Her earlier&nbsp;
monologue implied that could be on the horizon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.660
But for the film's final joke, her destination&nbsp;
is revealed to be a gynecologist's office.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:04.920
"Here I go."

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.840
At numerous points in the film, characters&nbsp;
including Mattel's CEO, Weird Barbie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.460
and Ruth the creator offer up crucial&nbsp;
pieces of lore laden with the burning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.500
necessity for follow-up questions, only to hand&nbsp;
wave them away. So when the film concludes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.480
Barbie turning from a doll to a real human&nbsp;
being, Ruth's phrasing implies that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.880
last climactic moment wasn't alchemy, but rather&nbsp;
the final act of a transformative character arc.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:34.560
Barbie goes from being a one-dimensional and&nbsp;
idealized toy whose world has never known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.820
struggle, contradiction or the finality&nbsp;
that defines a human life to becoming a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:44.040
thinking and feeling entity who endures the&nbsp;
full spectrum of existence all in a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:48.300
day. By pulling Barbie out of the fictional&nbsp;
and aspirational artifice she was born into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:54.240
and placing her within our very real world and&nbsp;
all its complications, she ascends from dollhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.620
to womanhood, bearing the massive weight&nbsp;
her gender must shoulder to simply exist.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:02.820
It's easier to believe Barbie became&nbsp;
human by virtue of magical transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.460
like Pinocchio, and that's a valid reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:10.860
of what happens. But it's also clear that a&nbsp;
more pragmatic explanation is just as valid.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.060
Just like the true nature of Barbie's&nbsp;
final evolution, the uneasy relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.740
between Barbieland and the real world is&nbsp;
interesting to ponder. We're told, initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:24.060
that what happens to the dolls in Barbieland&nbsp;
is the direct result of what the kids in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:29.160
real world do during playtime. So why then, is&nbsp;
Mattel so afraid of a doll in the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.020
and why does that give Barbieland similar&nbsp;
power over reality? Before Barbie is a physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:39.660
plaything in the real world, she is an idea — a&nbsp;
big one — housed within a malleable plastic totem.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:44.280
When Sasha lashes out at Barbie, all the&nbsp;
particulars of her scathing rant outline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:49.080
the many ways that Barbie, the doll, shapes&nbsp;
reality. The doll has become an ideal that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:54.000
on some level, flesh and blood women must&nbsp;
be compared to. How can you compete with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.720
an object designed to be perfect when&nbsp;
you're a human being who is, by nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:03.180
imperfect? We shape popular culture, but we imbue&nbsp;
art and products with the power to shape us too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:08.760
So of course the figures scrubbed clean of all&nbsp;
our ungainly humanity bounce right back at us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:13.200
reshaping the world as we know it in ways&nbsp;
surprising and previously unimaginable.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:18.240
It's also why the ideas Ken brings back from the&nbsp;
real world wreak so much havoc on Barbieland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:24.060
Barbieland's idyllic paradise is informed&nbsp;
entirely by a fragile barricade of lofty tenets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:28.080
utterly undone the moment they come into&nbsp;
contact with contradictory concepts from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.340
the broken world that initially birthed&nbsp;
them. "Barbie," miraculously, is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:38.340
expand upon the reflexive nature of the dream and&nbsp;
the dreamer, neither knowing who is truly asleep.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:39.900
"Ideas live forever."

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.880
Given director Greta Gerwig's unbridled&nbsp;
enthusiasm for the brand and the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:50.040
commercial success thus far, sequels to "Barbie"&nbsp;
are more a question of "when" rather than "if."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:55.500
There are plenty of Barbies and Kens to follow&nbsp;
into their own similarly meta dramedy vehicles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:59.520
that evolve from the blueprint set out in&nbsp;
the first film. Too many talented young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.480
actors appear here for one of them to not&nbsp;
get another shot as their respective doll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:08.640
identities. But with Mattel pledging to exploit&nbsp;
all the other toys in their massive IP toybox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.320
there may be a larger Mattel cinematic universe&nbsp;
that Barbie would merely be a player in.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.760
How, exactly, could Barbie team up with&nbsp;
He-Man, or the cars from Hot Wheels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:22.980
or Rock 'Em Sock 'Em robots? If you've made it&nbsp;
this far and have already seen "Barbie," you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:27.840
that the doors are blown wide open for any manner&nbsp;
of tie-in to make some kind of emotional sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:33.300
even if it’s found to be wanting in the logic&nbsp;
department. Barbie could end up in Eternia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:37.860
and the right combination of winking, nodding,&nbsp;
and sharply employed '80s or '90s needle drops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:42.300
on the soundtrack will smooth over any rockiness&nbsp;
between the various brands' respective identities.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:47.760
Reviews of "Barbie" have called it movie of the&nbsp;
summer, as well as one of the best of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.180
highlighting Gerwig and co-writer&nbsp;
Noah Baumbach's script as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:54.600
as her direction. But where will&nbsp;
things go next? Robbie told Time,

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.120
"It could go a million different&nbsp;
directions from this point."

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:02.100
Gerwig expressed enthusiasm about a potential&nbsp;
"Barbie" franchise in an interview with Variety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:06.360
but there are no solid plans. Whether&nbsp;
or not Gerwig herself will return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.260
the director's chair is a more difficult&nbsp;
call, given the rumors around her and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:14.700
"Narnia" universe over at Netflix, but a&nbsp;
more tantalizing question is whether the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.940
Barbieverse will cross over with&nbsp;
another toy-birthed franchise.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:22.560
This summer, we've already seen that Hasbro&nbsp;
and Paramount's twin-toy juggernauts "GI Joe"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.940
and "Transformers" are joining forces, and&nbsp;
we know Mattel and Hasbro are buddy-buddy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:30.000
enough when it comes to mutually&nbsp;
beneficial licensing opportunities;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.960
they're even planning a Barbie Monopoly&nbsp;
game. Who's to say the "real world"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:38.460
Barbie has entered isn't the same one&nbsp;
with robots that turn into cars and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:42.480
stealth ninjas working with independent&nbsp;
military contractors to fight terrorism?

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:45.480
"Yeah, I'm…confused about that."

